1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,005
Yesterday, the clock started.

2
00:00:02,005 --> 00:00:03,632
Today, look closer.

3
00:00:03,632 --> 00:00:05,690
Your vial doesn't have one clock.

4
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:06,783
It has two.

5
00:00:06,783 --> 00:00:08,619
Clock one: chemistry.

6
00:00:08,619 --> 00:00:11,640
In solution, the peptide slowly changes.

7
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:16,041
Reactions like hydrolysis and oxidation never fully stop.

8
00:00:16,041 --> 00:00:17,877
Cold slows this clock down.

9
00:00:17,877 --> 00:00:19,401
But it never resets.

10
00:00:19,401 --> 00:00:21,523
Clock two: microbiology.

11
00:00:21,523 --> 00:00:25,611
Every time a vial is opened, contamination becomes possible.

12
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:28,645
Preservatives help suppress bacterial growth.

13
00:00:28,645 --> 00:00:30,260
They don't stop the chemistry.

14
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:31,393
Two clocks.

15
00:00:31,393 --> 00:00:32,721
Two different risks.

16
00:00:32,721 --> 00:00:34,531
One changes the molecule.

17
00:00:34,531 --> 00:00:36,445
One contaminates the vial.

18
00:00:36,445 --> 00:00:37,734
And here's the trap.

19
00:00:37,734 --> 00:00:39,583
The famous twenty-eight days?

20
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:42,682
That number belongs to the microbiology clock.

21
00:00:42,682 --> 00:00:45,260
It says nothing about your peptide's chemistry.

22
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:46,354
That's tomorrow.

23
00:00:46,354 --> 00:00:47,656
Day two of ten.

24
00:00:47,656 --> 00:00:49,062
Both clocks are running.

25
00:00:49,062 --> 00:00:51,093
Follow so you don't miss day three.

